  "content": {
    "id": "p7-q3-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "3",
    "prompt": "Using the null factor law, write down the solutions to these equations.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p7-q3-ab",
        "type": "group",
        "label": "",
        "prompt": "",
        "layout": "grid",
        "columns": 2,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [
          {
            "id": "p7-q3-a",
            "type": "part",
            "label": "a",
            "prompt": "$(x-1)(x+2)=0$",
            "layout": "list",
            "columns": null,
            "questionDiagrams": [],
            "children": [],
            "answer": {
              "short": "$x=1$ or $x=-2$.",
              "worked": "By the null factor law, $x-1=0$ or $x+2=0$.\n$$x=1\\quad\\text{or}\\quad x=-2$$",
              "solutionDiagrams": []
            },
            "answerSpaceMm": 18
          },
          {
            "id": "p7-q3-b",
            "type": "part",
            "label": "b",
            "prompt": "$2(x-1)(x+2)=0$",
            "layout": "list",
            "columns": null,
            "questionDiagrams": [],
            "children": [],
            "answer": {
              "short": "$x=1$ or $x=-2$.",
              "worked": "The factor $2$ cannot be zero. By the null factor law, $x-1=0$ or $x+2=0$.\n$$x=1\\quad\\text{or}\\quad x=-2$$",
              "solutionDiagrams": []
            },
            "answerSpaceMm": 18
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "p7-q3-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "With reference to the null factor law, explain why a common factor of 2 does not change the answer in the two examples above.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Dividing both sides by $2$ gives an equivalent equation, so the solutions stay the same.",
          "worked": "The constant factor $2$ is nonzero, so the null factor law still requires $x-1=0$ or $x+2=0$. Equivalently, dividing both sides by $2$ gives $(x-1)(x+2)=0$, with the same solutions.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 18
      },
      {
        "id": "p7-q3-d",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "d",
        "prompt": "Solve $x^2-5x-6=0$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=6$ or $x=-1$.",
          "worked": "The required factors have product $-6$ and sum $-5$: $-6$ and $1$.\n$$\\begin{align*}x^2-5x-6&=0\\\\(x-6)(x+1)&=0\\end{align*}$$\nBy the null factor law, $x-6=0$ or $x+1=0$.\n$$x=6\\quad\\text{or}\\quad x=-1$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 28
      },
      {
        "id": "p7-q3-e",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "e",
        "prompt": "Hence solve $3x^2-15x-18=0$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$x=6$ or $x=-1$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}3(x^2-5x-6)&=0\\\\x^2-5x-6&=0\\end{align*}$$\nDividing by the nonzero constant $3$ preserves the solutions. From part d, $x=6$ or $x=-1$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 26
      }
    ]
  },
